            It can be shown by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/currying"} currying
            that on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+closed+category"} monoidal closed category
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Kock71"} Kock ‘71, Section 2
          %h3#tensor_product_of_algebras_and_multimorphisms Tensor product of algebras and multimorphisms
          %p
            In many situations,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebras
            over a commutative monad are canonically equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            analogous to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of modules
            over a ring. In particular, commutative monads come equipped with a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multimorphisms"} multimorphisms
            of algebras, analogous to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear"} bilinear
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multilinear+maps"} multilinear maps
          %p
            On a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} monoidal closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint"} adjoint
            to each other, making the category of algebra itself monoidal closed. This, once again, generalizes the hom-tensor adjunction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
          %p
            For the details, see
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+algebras+over+a+commutative+monad"} tensor product of algebras over a commutative monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+algebras+over+a+commutative+monad#bimorphisms_and_universal_property"} section on the universal property
            for multimorphisms, as well as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom+of+algebras+over+a+commutative+monad"} internal hom of algebras over a commutative monad
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            In many of the examples, one can see how the property of commutativity (as opposed to just the structure of a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monad"} strength
          %ul
            %li
              The free
              %em commutative
              monoid monad on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              is commutative, the free monoid monad is not.
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+monad"} action monad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi M
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\mapsto M\times X
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              is commutative if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is commutative as a monoid.
            %li
              More generally, in an arbitrary
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+monad"} action monad
              induced by tensoring with an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+monoid"} internal monoid
              is commutative if and only the monoid object is commutative. (See for example
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "#Brandenburg2014"} Brandenburg, Example 6.3.12
          %p Since commutative monads are the same as monoidal monads, monads encoding structures which admit products are commutative:
          %ul
            %li
              Most
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} probability monads
              are commutative, with monoidal structure given by forming the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+probability"} product probability
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
              monad is commutative, with monoidal structure given by forming the product of subsets.
          %p
            For a concrete example, consider the free commutative monoid monad
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
